@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the examination of the head of the queue
#
# SPEC §16 makes the untested generalizations the queue for the examining
# layer, ordered by the number of attested claims under them and the spread of
# their traditions. pmc:DistantPerceptionFromAVoluntaryState has headed that
# queue since the folder was made, and says so at its own node. This is its
# protocol.
#
# The examination was possible at all because of how that generalization is
# worded. It does not say that the capacity exists; it says that reports made
# from a deliberately entered state agree with established facts more often
# than reports made from the same prior information without the state. That is
# a comparison, and a comparison is a thing a modern literature can have run.
# It has run it. Four times in sixty years, by the count below, and the four
# do not agree.
#

pmc:DistantPerceptionAgainstAControlProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the state beats a control condition on the same task"@en, "Ob der Zustand eine Kontrollbedingung bei derselben Aufgabe schlägt"@de ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the comparison clause: not whether free-response reports about distant targets exceed chance, but whether reports made from a deliberately entered inner state exceed reports made without that state, at the same task and from the same prior information."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:DistantPerceptionFromAVoluntaryState ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "An experiment in which the same participants perform the same free-response task, with the same target pool and the same judging procedure, once in the named state and once in a condition differing from it only by the absence of that state, and in which the state condition does not score higher than the control condition."@en ;
    pm:baseRate "The score of the control condition at the same task. In the four-choice free-response design that carries most of this literature, chance is 25 percent by construction, and the standard free-response database of the meta-analysis read here returns 24.3 percent over 1,872 trials, which is the empirical control figure rather than the arithmetic one."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The comparison is the design of van Dalen, Dias, Murre and Schouten, who state the requirement in their own words, and the category contrast is the first hypothesis of Storm, Tressoldi and Di Risio" ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "L. Storm, P. E. Tressoldi and L. Di Risio, Meta-analysis of free-response studies, 1992-2008: Assessing the noise reduction model in parapsychology, Psychological Bulletin 136(4) (2010), pp. 471-485, doi:10.1037/a0019457" ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "L. Storm and P. E. Tressoldi, Meta-analysis of free-response studies 2009-2018: Assessing the noise-reduction model ten years on, Journal of the Society for Psychical Research 84(4) (2020), pp. 193-219, read in the author preprint doi:10.31234/osf.io/3d7at" ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "A. C. van Dalen, L. R. B. Dias, J. M. J. Murre and S. A. Schouten, A prototypical ganzfeld psi experiment with a control condition, Parapsychology Laboratory, University of Utrecht, convention abstract, pp. 40-43, read in the declassified copy CIA-RDP96-00792R000700960003-3" ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "J. N. Rouder, R. D. Morey and J. M. Province, A Bayes factor meta-analysis of recent extrasensory perception experiments: Comment on Storm, Tressoldi, and Di Risio (2010), Psychological Bulletin 139(1) (2013), pp. 241-247, doi:10.1037/a0029008" ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "J. Milton and R. Wiseman, Does psi exist? Lack of replication of an anomalous process of information transfer, Psychological Bulletin 125(4) (1999), pp. 387-391" ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Carried on 2026-09-20 and it found opposition of three kinds, each read at the wording. First, a direct null: the only experiment found that runs the comparison within subjects reports no difference between the two conditions, and no effect in either of them. Second, a methodological attack on the one meta-analysis that does draw the contrast: a Bayes factor analysis of the same data set finds that studies with manual randomisation outperform those with computerised randomisation by a factor of about 6,350 to 1, which the authors read as a flaw in the manual studies rather than as an effect, and that two conditions from included reports were left out of the meta-analysis. Third, a large replication series that fails the underlying main effect altogether: thirty ganzfeld studies from seven laboratories following the agreed standards, Stouffer z = 0.70, p = .24 one-tailed, mean effect size 0.013. What the search did not find is a preregistered multi-laboratory experiment with a control condition; none appears to exist."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-20" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:protocolledPractice ;
    skos:note "The case for the statement, and it is one case. Storm, Tressoldi and Di Risio sorted free-response studies from 1992 to 2008 into three groups by the state of the receiver: ganzfeld, other noise-reduction techniques such as hypnosis, meditation, relaxation and dreaming, and standard free response with no state induction at all. The three groups take the same task and the same chance level. Ganzfeld returned a mean effect size of 0.142 and, on trial counts, 483 hits in 1,498 trials, a 32.2 percent hit rate; the standard free-response group returned a mean effect size of -0.029 and 455 hits in 1,872 trials, a 24.3 percent hit rate. The contrast is tested and not merely displayed: an analysis of variance gives F(2, 53) = 6.08, p = .004 two-tailed, and a Tukey test gives the difference between those two groups at p = .005. That is the comparison clause, supported, at pp. 475 to 477 of the article."@en ;
    skos:note "The first deviation is inside that same article and is not mentioned in its abstract. The category difference does not hold for the participants the transmissions would be about. Splitting by participant type, for unselected participants there is no difference between the three conditions, F(2, 27) = 1.44, p = .255; for participants selected as believers, meditators and the like there is, F(2, 26) = 6.83, p = .004, with the difference again between ganzfeld and standard free response. The authors state it themselves: the effect is attributable to participants in the ganzfeld only, and within the ganzfeld selected participants beat unselected ones, t(27) = -3.44, p = .002. So the state advantage in this data set is not separable from who was in the state. The generalization says a practitioner enters a state; it does not say that only practitioners already disposed to succeed do so."@en ;
    skos:note "The second deviation is the decade that followed. The same two authors carried the same three-way comparison forward with studies from 2009 to 2018 and reported the outcome against themselves. In the new data alone the standard free-response category, which is the control, returns a mean effect size of 0.050 while the non-ganzfeld state category returns 0.045 — the control outperforms the state. On the combined databases the control category returns 0.027 with a 95 percent confidence interval of -0.024 to 0.078, which includes zero, and the analysis of variance on the categories falls to F(2, 74) = 2.58, p = .082. The ganzfeld-versus-control contrast survives in the post hoc Tukey test at p = .012 and becomes clear again only in a reduced model that drops the other factors. A contrast that was p = .004 in 2010 and p = .082 in 2020, on more data, is not a finding that has been strengthened by replication."@en ;
    skos:note "The third deviation is the only experiment this examination found that runs the comparison the statement actually describes: the same people, the same task, with and without the state. Forty-one participants, each tested once in a ganzfeld condition and once in a condition equal to it in every respect except that the halved ping-pong balls, the red light, the headphones and the white noise were absent; the order of conditions randomised across participants; one target pool of eighty pictures; a hardware random number generator; a three-experimenter plan; and the methodological criticism of the preceding decade worked into the design on purpose. The success criterion was fixed in advance and had two parts, a significant difference between conditions and significant hitting in the ganzfeld condition alone. The result: no difference between the conditions, Wilcoxon T = 344.5, z = -0.11, p greater than .90, and no evidence of psi in either condition."@en ;
    skos:note "That study has to be read exactly, because it is the one most easily overstated in either direction. The authors themselves say what it does and does not settle: since no effect appeared in either condition, they cannot reach even a tentative conclusion about a differential advantage of the ganzfeld. A comparison between two nulls is uninformative about which would be larger if either were positive. What it does establish is that the comparison has been attempted with a design built for it, and that it did not come out; and that its own post hoc finding, a correlation of hits across the two conditions, points away from the method mattering and toward the person."@en ;
    skos:note "The fourth line of evidence does not bear on the comparison at all but decides how much the supporting side is worth. A Bayes factor reanalysis of the very data set that carries the category contrast finds that studies randomising by hand outperform studies randomising by computer by about 6,350 to 1, and reads that as a flaw in the manual studies rather than as an effect, since no anomaly is needed to produce dependencies between trials that a hand procedure leaves in. With those studies excluded and two omitted conditions restored, the support for any psi effect in the set falls to at most 330 to 1."@en ;
    skos:note "One of those two omitted conditions is the sharpest single fact this examination turned up, and it is a fact about the comparison and not about psi. An experiment in the meta-analysis ran two conditions, one under hypnosis and one without: 45 hits in 120 four-choice trials under hypnosis, 37.5 percent, and 29 hits in 120 trials without it, 24.2 percent. The meta-analysis included the hypnosis condition and not the control. The one study in the set that had built the state comparison into a single experiment therefore entered the category contrast with only its state arm. Whether that was deliberate is not decided here and does not need to be: a meta-analysis that sorts studies by the state of the receiver, and takes one arm of a within-study state comparison, has measured something other than what it reports."@en ;
    skos:note "What the examination does not find, and the absence is the reason the state is deviation rather than refutation. It does not find a body of work that has tested the comparison and come out against it. It finds one direct test, null in both arms; one between-studies contrast that supported it in 2010 and weakened to marginal by 2020; a reanalysis that removes most of the weight from the supporting side; and, beneath all of it, a main effect whose own replication record is contested — thirty studies from seven laboratories under the agreed standards giving Stouffer z = 0.70, p = .24. The generalization is not refuted. It is close to untested, and the literature that looks as though it tested it mostly did not: the canonical series compares performance against chance, which is a different question with a similar shape."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence for the generalization itself is a wording change and not a withdrawal, and it is proposed here rather than made, because the statement belongs to the node and this is its protocol. The clause people who have not entered such a state is doing more work than the evidence supports: in the one data set where the contrast holds, it holds only for participants selected for belief or practice, which means the contrast in hand is between selected-people-in-a-state and unselected-people-not-in-one. A statement that named that would be examinable by an experiment that exists in principle and, as far as this examination can tell, has been run once."@en ;
    skos:note "A note on the evidence that could not be opened, because the state of this protocol depends on it. The two founding papers of the controversy, the critical appraisal of 1985 and the reply to it, and the joint communique of 1986, are in a journal with no open access and were not read; what this protocol says about them is therefore nothing. The same holds for the meta-analysis of free-response studies without altered states of 1997, whose reported effect size is higher than the ganzfeld figure of the study read here and which would bear directly on the comparison. A later run with access to that journal should read those four before the state above is moved in either direction."@en .
